      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "Comparison of conjunctival closure with fibrin glue and conventional closure in strabismus surgeries."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty-nine patients undergoing strabismus surgery were studied. Group 1 included twelve cases in whom Tisseel was used for closure and group 2 included seventeen cases in whom 7/0 Vicryl was used. Severity of redness, watering, pain, and discomfort in opening eyes were evaluated postoperatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In the first hour, severe pain was experienced in both groups without statistical significance; but in group 1, pain was less severe in the 12th and 24th hour (P < .0001). After the first hour severity of watering was less in group 1 (P < .0001). Discomfort in opening eyes was detected in fifteen patients in group 2 in the first 12 hours where only one case with fibrin glue had discomfort. In the second week, no differences in severity of complaints were seen."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Conjunctival closure with Tisseel following strabismus surgery might be an alternative to suturing."
        }
      ]},
